msm6295: save the render position and interpolated samples in savestates

MSM6295Render() advances each chip's channels by nFractionalPosition, and interpolates
between nPreviousSample and nCurrentSample, all carried from one frame into the next;
MSM6295Scan() saved none of them. A loaded state then advanced the channels at points that
depended on the history before the load, so a sample ended, and the status the sound CPU
reads changed, a few samples earlier or later than in the run that saved the state. In
Battle Garegga's play the channels diverged about 1850 frames after a load and the Z80 RAM
about 3300 frames after it (docs/performance.md, "FBNeo and savestates").

Upstream: not merged yet; drop this file when the pinned commit contains the change
(docs/cores.md, "Patches").

diff --git a/src/burn/snd/msm6295.cpp b/src/burn/snd/msm6295.cpp
index 99e3de3..b24e1bb 100644
--- a/src/burn/snd/msm6295.cpp
+++ b/src/burn/snd/msm6295.cpp
@@ -46,7 +46,7 @@ struct MSM6295Struct {
 	bool bIsCommand;
 	INT32 nSampleInfo;
 
-	// Not scanned.
+	// Not scanned, except nFractionalPosition (MSM6295Scan).
 	INT32 nVolume;
 	INT32 nOutputDir;
 	INT32 nSampleRate;
@@ -157,6 +157,12 @@ void MSM6295Scan(INT32 , INT32 *)
 	{
 		ScanVar(&MSM6295[nChip], STRUCT_SIZE_HELPER(struct MSM6295Struct, nSampleInfo), "MSM6295 Chip");
 		SCAN_VAR(nMSM6295Status[nChip]);
+		// The render position and the samples it interpolates between. Without them a
+		// loaded state ends samples, and so changes the status the sound CPU reads, at
+		// points that depend on the history before the load.
+		SCAN_VAR(MSM6295[nChip].nFractionalPosition);
+		SCAN_VAR(nPreviousSample[nChip]);
+		SCAN_VAR(nCurrentSample[nChip]);
 	}
 }
 
